    "created_at": "2023-03-27T06:23:37Z",
    "updated_at": "2023-03-27T06:49:39Z",
    "body": "> Is this necessary? Truncation symbol \".../\" is already converted to \"...\\\" on windows.\r\n\r\nUnless I\"m missing something, it is. You can't use `/` or `\\` inside the `truncation_symbol` variable (which is very handy) as they won't be converted, even though the slash is converted to backslash internally.\r\n\r\nAt the moment I have to resort to use the following:\r\n```\r\ntruncation_symbol = \"\\ue5ff/\" (linux)\r\ntruncation_symbol = \"\\ue5ff\\\\\" (windows)\r\n```\r\n\r\nMaybe a better implementation than having a variable would be to autoconvert all slash to backslash when found in other variable, without the need to introduce a new `$pathsep` variable. ",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
